1
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:05,980
What does a software team actually produce?

2
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:10,590
In 1985, Peter Naur's surprising answer: not the code.

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:13,800
Programming, he argued, is theory building:

4
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:15,360
working out how part of the world

5
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:17,020
will be handled by a program.

6
00:00:17,290 --> 00:00:19,950
Code and documents, even specs, come second.

7
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:22,390
It's theory in Gilbert Ryle's sense:

8
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:25,150
knowing how, and being able to explain it.

9
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:27,810
Whoever holds it can map the program to the world,

10
00:00:27,830 --> 00:00:30,890
justify each part, and judge which changes fit.

11
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,120
Naur saw successive teams inherit a compiler,

12
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:36,220
with full code and full docs,

13
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:38,240
and still patch its design apart.

14
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:39,760
The theory didn't travel.

15
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:41,390
And when its holders leave,

16
00:00:41,410 --> 00:00:43,890
a program dies, even while it still runs.

17
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:45,990
Now agents write the code.

18
00:00:46,190 --> 00:00:47,650
Text is nearly free.

19
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:50,670
But for Naur, text was never the expensive part.

20
00:00:50,770 --> 00:00:51,730
The theory is.

21
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:53,940
And specifying is how you build it.

22
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:56,560
The agent gets your spec. You keep the theory.

23
00:00:57,010 --> 00:00:58,610
In a regulated enterprise,

24
00:00:58,710 --> 00:01:02,150
that theory is scattered: a rule buried in policy,

25
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:04,590
a platform quirk one engineer knows,

26
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:06,810
a constraint nobody says out loud.

27
00:01:07,070 --> 00:01:09,390
Requirements gathering is theory building.

28
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,180
So: trace every requirement to its source.

29
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:14,530
Write down the why, and what you rejected.

30
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:17,970
Rehearse change: does a new rule fit, or need a patch?

31
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:20,330
And sit with the people who hold the theory.

32
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:22,590
Keep them in the loop as agents build.

33
00:01:23,290 --> 00:01:26,150
Code is the output. The spec is the handoff.

34
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:27,530
The theory is the product.
